  "title": "rumah saya — possession",
  "pattern": "thing + owner: rumah saya · rumahnya · punya siapa?",
  "explanation": "To say whose something is, put the owner after the thing: rumah saya is 'my house' (literally 'house I'), and mobil teman saya is 'my friend's car'. There are no separate words for my, your or 's: the pronoun or name does the job, so nama Ibu is 'your name' to an older woman. For 'his' or 'her', people usually put -nya on the end instead of dia: namanya (his name, her name). And punya siapa? asks 'whose?': Ini punya siapa? (Whose is this?)",
  "natural": "With friends, -ku and -mu on the end are the casual my and your: rumahku (my house), namamu (your name). Namanya siapa? is also a friendly, polite way to ask someone's own name.",
  "stiff": "English order gives the wrong meaning: saya rumah doesn't mean 'my house', and 'my friend's car' is mobil teman saya, with the owner last. Keep -ku and -mu for friends: to your partner's mother, rumahmu is far too familiar, so say rumah Ibu.",
